    "short_note": "WEB says Jesus gave an “example.” Greek ὑπόδειγμα can also describe a pattern, model, or demonstration. Verse 14 has already said that the disciples “ought” to wash one another’s feet, so the example carries an explicit obligation.",
    "full_note_paragraphs": [
      "WEB renders ὑπόδειγμα as “example.” The noun can also describe a pattern, model, or demonstration that shows what another person should do.",
      "The obligation doesn’t depend on choosing “pattern” instead of “example.” In verse 14, Jesus has already said that the disciples ought to wash one another’s feet. Verse 15 then points to his action as the pattern they are to follow.",
      "The passage supports concrete imitation and self-lowering service. It doesn’t by itself settle whether footwashing must function as a recurring ordinance."
    ],
    "interpretive_limits": [
      "The noun alone doesn’t decide later ordinance debates.",
      "The wider pattern shouldn’t erase Jesus’ concrete command to wash one another’s feet.",
      "“Pattern” is an explanatory rendering, not the only possible English gloss."
    ],
